## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant data delivery with cache layers and maintenance resilience are common in production APIs. This question checks practical testing habits for correctness, isolation, performance boundaries, and failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation and authorization checks
- Cache layering and eviction strategies
- Data-change invalidation triggers
- Failure mode handling and graceful degradation
- Latency verification across hot/cold paths

## Code Example

```javascript
// Jest + supertest example for tenant isolation
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

test('403 when tenant mismatch', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .get('/reports/tenantA/report1')
    .set('Authorization', 'Bearer token-for-tenantB');
  expect(res.status).toBe(403);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you automate cache eviction verification after a data update?
- How would you simulate Redis outages and verify fallback behavior in tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming API behavior under realistic conditions, including security, multi-tenant isolation, delivery guarantees, backpressure, and fault scenarios. It reveals depth in end-to-end testing of real-time systems.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket end-to-end testing
- JWT-based tenant isolation
- Ordering, deduplication, and reconnect semantics
- Backpressure and flow control
- Fault injection and resiliency testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example WebSocket test skeleton using ws library
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

// Test client for WebSocket validation
const testWebSocketConnection = async (tenantId, token) => {
  const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://localhost/ws/updates?token=${token}`);

  ws.on('open', () => {
    console.log(`Connected for tenant: ${tenantId}`);
  });

  ws.on('message', (data) => {
    // Validate tenant isolation and message ordering
    const message = JSON.parse(data);
    console.log(`Received: ${message.sequenceId} for tenant: ${tenantId}`);
  });

  return ws;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time, asynchronous, event-driven integrations with external partners are inherently error-prone. This question evaluates understanding of contract testing, schema evolution strategy, latency SLAs, idempotency, and resilience patterns—all critical for payment infrastructure reliability.

## Key Concepts

- Consumer-driven contracts (Pact) for multi-party API agreements
- Backward-compatible schema evolution with feature-flagged payloads
- Async webhook reliability: retries, dead-letter queues, circuit breakers
- End-to-end latency measurement with OpenTelemetry distributed tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pact contract for webhook payload versioning
const { Pact } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');

const provider = new Pact({
  consumer: 'fraud-gateway',
  provider: 'partner-webhook',
  cors: true,
});

describe('Partner Webhook Contract', () => {
  before(() => provider.setup());
  after(() => provider.finalize());

  it('accepts v2 schema with optional risk fields', async () => {
    await provider.addInteraction({
      state: 'partner supports v2 schema',
      uponReceiving: 'a fraud score webhook',
      withRequest: {
        method: 'POST',
        path: '/partners/123/webhook',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
        body: {
          transactionId: 'txn_abc123',
          riskScore: 85,
          newField: 'feature-flag-value', // v2-only field
        },
      },
      willRespondWith: {
        status: 200,
        body: { received: true },
      },
    });
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end testing of a distributed transaction orchestrator, focusing on idempotency, compensation, and eventual consistency—critical in payments-like flows.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Distributed orchestration and compensating actions
- Partial-failure handling and rollback paths
- End-to-end vs contract tests with downstream services
- Observability and deterministic test data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test skeleton for Node.js (mocha)
describe('POST /payments idempotent flow', () => {
  it('preserves a single transaction for repeated calls with same idempotency key', async () => {
    const key = 'idem-123';
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    await postPayment({amount: 100}, key);
    const txs = await listPaymentsByKey(key);
    assert.strictEqual(txs.length, 1);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate a bank service outage during the transaction and verify idempotence?
